# .loom/resync-ignore — paths Loom must not overwrite or delete.
#
# Format: one `.loom/`-relative path per line. Blank lines and `#` comments are
# ignored; matching is exact (no globs). See `.loom/docs/repo-owned-files.md`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# hooks/guard-destructive-generic.sh — repo#441 (ports repo#439)
#
# WHY THIS IS PINNED
#
# repo#439 fixed the CANONICAL guard (`hooks/repo/guard-destructive.sh`, PR
# #442): a write into the main checkout wrapped in a quoted SINGLE-command
# `$( … )` / backtick substitution now DENIES under
# `worktree-write-confinement`. Loom's vendored copy of the generic guard
# still allowed it — and the vendored copy is the one a Loom-managed repo
# runs BEFORE the capability probe flips it to the canonical copy, so the
# escape was live there:
#
#     echo "$(id > <main-checkout>/evil.sh)"     # shell really performs it
#     echo "$(cp /tmp/s <main-checkout>/evil.sh)"
#     echo "`id > <main-checkout>/evil.sh`"
#
# `.loom/docs/repo-owned-files.md` gives exactly two durable dispositions for
# a bug in an installed Loom file: **upstream it** to `rjwalters/loom`'s
# `defaults/` tree, or **pin it** here. Upstreaming was attempted first and is
# not reachable from this repo's automation:
#
#     gh repo view rjwalters/loom --json viewerPermission  ->  ""  (no access)
#     gh api user                                          ->  403 Resource not
#                                                              accessible by
#                                                              integration
#
# This repo's `gh` credential is a GitHub App INSTALLATION token (`ghs_…`)
# scoped to this repository. It has no user context, so it can neither fork
# `rjwalters/loom` nor open a cross-repo PR from a fork. That leaves the pin
# as the only disposition an automated Builder can actually complete, and the
# fix is a hard-deny write-confinement escape that should not wait on a human
# forking round-trip.
#
# COST OF THIS PIN, STATED PLAINLY
#
# The file is now repo-owned: `resync-installed.sh` and the installer's clean
# sweep will never refresh it from upstream again, so upstream fixes to the
# generic guard stop arriving here automatically.
#
# HOW TO RETIRE IT
#
# 1. Open a PR against `rjwalters/loom`'s
#    `defaults/hooks/guard-destructive-generic.sh` carrying the repo#441 port
#    (`bs_escaped()` / `subst_depth()` / `subst_heads()` and the
#    `_extract_write_targets_scan()` wiring — search that file for `repo#439`).
# 2. Once it merges and a `chore: resync installed Loom surfaces` commit lands
#    a vendored copy that already denies the three shapes above, DELETE this
#    stanza and let the file resync normally again.
# 3. `commands/repo/tests/test-guard-equivalence.sh` is the regression net for
#    step 2: if a resync ever reverts the port, the three `wt:` corpus rows
#    (`guard-equivalence-cases.txt:256-258`) come back as UNDECLARED stricter
#    divergences and say so by name.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
hooks/guard-destructive-generic.sh
